<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Dev override (docs/architecture/repositories.md): set the AgoPlatformDevOverride environment
    variable to swap Ago.Platform.* PackageReferences for ProjectReferences into a sibling
    ../ago-platform checkout, for a change that spans both repositories.

      AgoPlatformDevOverride=true dotnet build            (bash)
      $env:AgoPlatformDevOverride = 'true'; dotnet build  (PowerShell)

    Nothing in this repository changes when the override is used, so a merged branch cannot
    accidentally keep it switched on - CI never sets the variable and always builds against the
    published package.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AgoPlatformDevOverride Condition="'$(AgoPlatformDevOverride)' == ''">false</AgoPlatformDevOverride>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- `23-114`: keep only the native binaries a build could actually load.

       SkiaSharp's native-asset packages carry every platform they support, and a build with no
       `RuntimeIdentifier` copies all of them: 440 MB under `bin/Release/net10.0/runtimes/`, of which
       win-x86 (98 MB) and win-arm64 (93 MB) are for machines this project has never run on. Multiplied
       across worktrees that is tens of gigabytes; the workspace had reached 158 GB, 147 of it `bin`
       and `obj`.

       IT LIVES HERE, NOT IN Ago.Chat.Worker.csproj, AND THAT WAS FOUND BY MEASURING. Only the Worker
       references SkiaSharp, so putting the target beside that reference looked right and took the
       Worker's own output from 440 MB to 97 MB. The integration test project, which references the
       Worker, still wrote all 440 MB - native assets are resolved per project, so every project that
       transitively reaches the package copies its own full set. A repository-wide rule is what the
       rule actually is.

       WHAT IT DELIBERATELY CANNOT TOUCH. The container image comes from a RID-qualified,
       framework-dependent publish (see `Dockerfile`), and such a publish already resolves exactly one
       platform's assets, which is why that image is 67 MB and carries no `runtimes/` tree at all. The
       condition is `'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == ''`, so this cannot run on that path even by accident.

       WHY NOT `ExcludeAssets` ON THE PACKAGE. Dropping `SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Win32` would take
       win-x64 with it and break local `dotnet run` and the thumbnail tests, which is what that
       package is there for. The unwanted part is not the package, it is the platforms inside it.

       The rule is "the OS this build runs on, and on Windows that OS's architecture", read from the
       running process rather than hardcoded, so an arm64 machine keeps arm64 and loses x64 instead.
       Linux keeps every `linux-*` variant: a musl or bionic container is a plausible host for the same
       binaries, and the saving there is not what this is about.
  -->
  <Target Name="TrimUnloadableNativeRuntimes"
          AfterTargets="ResolveAssemblyReferences"
          Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == ''">
    <ItemGroup Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Windows'))">
      <_AgoKeepRid Include="win-$([System.Runtime.InteropServices.RuntimeInformation]::OSArchitecture.ToString().ToLowerInvariant())" />
      <_AgoUnloadable Include="@(RuntimeTargetsCopyLocalItems)"
                      Condition="'%(RuntimeTargetsCopyLocalItems.RuntimeIdentifier)' != '@(_AgoKeepRid)'" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Condition="!$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Windows'))">
      <_AgoUnloadable Include="@(RuntimeTargetsCopyLocalItems)"
                      Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(RuntimeTargetsCopyLocalItems.RuntimeIdentifier)').StartsWith('win'))
                              or $([System.String]::Copy('%(RuntimeTargetsCopyLocalItems.RuntimeIdentifier)').StartsWith('osx'))" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
      <RuntimeTargetsCopyLocalItems Remove="@(_AgoUnloadable)" />
      <ReferenceCopyLocalPaths Remove="@(_AgoUnloadable)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
